<!--
    An indexed store into an AArch64 stack array whose guarded slots lie past
    the four elements the stack layout gives an array whose window is not
    locked: the slots must be elements of the array the store writes
    through.  Before, the layout made b[6] its own local after a six-element
    array (or printed the store as `(&v1)[a1 & 7] = 3` into a scalar), so the
    C wrote past the array and returned the separate b[6]; for `long b[8]`
    gcc's x86 build of the printed C aborted with stack smashing.

    clang 14, target aarch64-linux-gnu, -O2 -fno-stack-protector, .text at
    0x100000:

        int l6b(int i, int j) { long b[8]; b[6] = i; b[1] = j; b[j & 7] = 3;
            return (int)(b[6] + b[1]); }
        int i6b(int i, int j) { int b[8]; b[6] = i; b[1] = j; b[j & 7] = 3;
            return b[6] + b[1]; }
        int u2(int i, int j) { long b[8]; b[1] = i; b[2] = i + 1; b[j] = 3;
            return (int)(b[1] + b[2]); }
        int u5(int i, int j) { int b[8]; b[1] = i; b[j] = 3; return (short)b[1]; }
        int w5(int i, int j) { long b[8]; b[3] = i; long *p = b; int n = j >> 1;
            while (n) { n -= 1; *p = n * 9 + 1; p += 2; }
            return (int)b[3] + (short)b[3]; }

    Stage: P3 heritage stackstoreguard guards b[6] and b[1] at the indexed
    store (its index is bounded by `& 7`), and the P6 layout makes the array
    at the store's base as long as that bound.  u2 and u5 index with no
    bound; their guarded slots lie inside the four elements the layout gives
    the array, and the narrower reads of them must not split it into scalars
    (`(&v1)[a1] = 3` past a scalar, or with an 8-byte stride for u5's ints).
    w5 walks the array with stride 2, which never reaches the guarded b[3];
    the guard must not end that array before b[3].  Pass 1 (default) reads both
    slots back after the store; pass 2 (stackstoreguard off) reproduces the
    stale values, as on main.
-->
